Falsifies `paging::aarch64::the_user_va_gate_admits_only_the_aligned_low_half`.

The defect: `is_user_page_va` drops the page-alignment conjunct and keeps only the
half test. Every honest caller passes an aligned address, so nothing in the system
behaves differently, and what the gate stops accepting is the hostile case: a user
program naming a virtual address a few bytes inside a page. A mapping request at
`va` and one at `va + 8` then both resolve to the same leaf, so a second map can
silently replace the first, and the arithmetic the walk does on an unaligned VA
was never meant to see one.

Expected to fail: `assert_eq!(is_user_page_va::<Aarch64>(va), va & 0xfff == 0 && va
>> 48 == 0)`, on any low-half address that is not page-aligned. The second
assertion in the harness, `assert!(is_in_half(Half::Low, va) && !is_in_half(Half::High,
va))`, still holds under this defect, because the half test is exactly what was
kept.

**The gate is shared and the proof is not**, which is the point of recording this
three times. `is_user_page_va` is one generic function in `lib.rs`; DECISIONS §19
makes each ISA state the property itself, so this one defect is caught three
times over. The `sv39` and `x86_64` records break the same function in two other
ways, so the three patches are not copies of each other.

diff --git a/crates/paging/src/page_format.rs b/crates/paging/src/page_format.rs
--- a/crates/paging/src/page_format.rs
+++ b/crates/paging/src/page_format.rs
@@ -40,7 +40,7 @@
 /// assert_eq!(PAGE_SIZE, 4096);
 /// ```
 pub fn is_user_page_va<F: PageFormat>(va: u64) -> bool {
-    F::is_in_half(Half::Low, va) && va.is_multiple_of(PAGE_SIZE)
+    F::is_in_half(Half::Low, va)
 }
 
 /// **The seam between the shared walk and a hardware page-table format.**
